WHERE id = 2579;

-- Q2581: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Kelly''s adjusted basis is $80,000 − $40,000 = $40,000. Her gain is $50,000 − $40,000 = $10,000. Under IRC Section 1245, gain on personal property is recaptured as ordinary income to the extent of prior depreciation ($40,000). Since the $10,000 gain is entirely within the depreciation claimed, all $10,000 is ordinary income.
Wrong (A): Section 1245 recapture takes priority over Section 1231 treatment. The gain cannot be classified as a Section 1231 gain until depreciation is fully recaptured.
Wrong (C): A $30,000 loss would result from subtracting the sale price from cost ($50,000 − $80,000), ignoring the $40,000 depreciation that reduced adjusted basis to $40,000.
Wrong (D): Long-term capital gain treatment is unavailable because Section 1245 recaptures the gain as ordinary income to the extent of prior depreciation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2581;

-- Q2582: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1211(b), individual taxpayers may deduct a maximum of $3,000 of net capital losses against ordinary income per year. Jennifer deducts $3,000 of her $4,000 loss in 2026, and the remaining $1,000 carries forward under Section 1212(b) as a long-term capital loss.
Wrong (A): $4,000 would mean the full loss is deductible, but Section 1211(b) caps the annual capital loss deduction at $3,000 for individuals.
Wrong (B): $0 is incorrect because capital losses are deductible up to $3,000 per year; they are not entirely disallowed.
Wrong (C): $1,000 is the carryforward amount, not the current-year deduction. The $3,000 limit is the maximum deductible amount, not $1,000.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2582;

-- Q2583: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): For business property, the casualty gain equals insurance proceeds minus adjusted basis: $310,000 − $200,000 = $110,000. Business casualty gains on property held over one year are Section 1231 gains, not subject to personal-use floors. This gain may be deferred under Section 1033 if the taxpayer reinvests in replacement property.
Wrong (A): A $40,000 loss would result from subtracting insurance from FMV ($350,000 − $310,000), but business casualty computations use adjusted basis, not FMV.
Wrong (C): $150,000 uses FMV minus basis ($350,000 − $200,000) and mislabels the result as an ordinary loss; the correct computation uses insurance proceeds minus basis.
Wrong (D): While the $110,000 amount is correct, the gain is classified as a Section 1231 gain, not simply a "casualty gain," because the property was used in a trade or business.$EXPL$
